Interface#h1

Ecmascript module loader for commands@./#Take_Command and procedures@./#Take_Control. 

2024


{text-align:justify;margin:0 10%;}
  Websites have become humanly unmaintainable. 

  No wonder artificial boilerplate is sweeping the industry. 

  Angular, React, Vue, Svelte, Astro, NextJS, NestJS, Commonjs, Typescript, Flow, JSX, NPM, pNPM, Yarn, Bun, Deno, Tailwind, GraphQL, (a list without end)@https://www.youtube.com/embed/xE9W9Ghe4Jk. The javascript platform has been increasingly convoluted by non-standard syntactic variations, not to mention semantic mayhem in its ecosystem, requiring growing amounts of configuration to know and maintain. Even when these have merit (static analysis, optimization), they are at the expense of javascript's freedom and flexibility, which could provide these capabilities natively. To make matters worse, package managers have long obscured configurations of external modules, contributing to overengineering on the web, and rendering unreadable a language that could as well be distributed in source format (so to servers as to browsers). 

  Does javascript really have to be this complicated? 


{text-align:justify;line-height:1.2em}

 (Take Command)#h2

 Since the arrival of loader commands in the (Ecmascript specification)@https://whatwg.github.io/loader/{"style":{"&+.media":{"filter":"invert(0.8)"}}} and their implementation in Node.js ((version 16)@https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#customization-hooks), volatile dialects can finally be standardized dynamically, without a need for external configurations or a new platform trying to contain this heterogeneity ((UMD)@https://jameshfisher.com/2020/10/04/what-are-umd-modules/, Deno@https://www.youtube.com/embed/M3BM9TB-8yA, Bun@https://bun.sh/docs). 

 Modules consist of imported#b, exported#b and internal declarations#b. Procedural statements are allowed due to javascript's lack of an interface to access modules otherwise. Interface#b corrects this with a single procedure that accesses their declarations with arguments provided on a (command line)#b. Take a simple module exporting a function to cumulate numbers: 

sum#[["/Blik_2023_inference.js/module/namespace/sum"],"interface/fetch","interface/digest",[["exports","sum"]],"inference/record",["module"],"meta/serialize",[{"source":"/Blik_2023_inference.js/module/namespace/sum","scale":0.8,"gutter":213}],"script","script/index"]{background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto;border-radius:.5em;border:2px dashed #616161}

 To invoke it, you wouldn't want to include a new procedure for every specific use case. Merging Interface among your files, it will infer them dynamically from specification on the command line: 

commandline#script/highlight(` git init;
 git remote add interface https://github.com/jsinterface/interface;
 git checkout interface/stable .;

 node ./Blik_2023_interface.js ./Blik_2023_inference.js sum 1 2 3;`){display:block;width:90%;margin:0px 5%;border-radius:.5em;border:1px solid #43a047;background:black;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;color:#616161;font-size:.8em}

 Without a command to infer, it will expose its own declarations for procedural use. Grab a terminal@https://termux.dev/, you might need it later: 

procedural#script/highlight(` node ./Blik_2023_interface.js;`){display:block;width:90%;margin:0px 5%;border-radius:.5em;border:1px solid #43a047;background:black;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;font-size:.8em;color:#616161}inference#[[
