 (Take Control)#h2

 (...of Conditions)#h3{display:inline;font-size:inherit}: 

 A command line is only one of many contexts a module may be loaded in, so the actual inference procedure depends on a specific set of their conditions. From the inside, modules have two pieces of information available about their external context: the "import"#b command carrying a (importmeta)#script/highlight(`{meta:{url}}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit} object with their own address, and the "global"#b object carrying peculiar others: (process)#script/highlight(`{process}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit} indicating Node, (window)#script/highlight(`{window}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit} a browser, or (imports)#script/highlight(`{imports}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit} a manually specified context. 
 
 After importing some general abstractions for semantics and syntax from the Inference and Meta modules, Interface uses these to derive its internal context of conditions. Its own name#b, location#b and protocol#b are promptly derived from the former, while the client agent#b ((agent)#script/highlight(`{node,browser,virtual}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit}) identified from the latter, along with corresponding language features#b ((features)#script/highlight(`{assertions,attributes,json}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit}) and command line options ((options)#script/highlight(`{loader,offload,inspected}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit}). Interpreter#b and thread#b rank are importable facilities in Node. 

conditions#[["/Blik_2023_interface.js"],"interface/fetch","interface/digest",[{"source":"/Blik_2023_interface.js","scale":0.8,"range":[0,16]}],"script","script/index"]{background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto;border-radius:.5em; border:2px dashed #616161}#[[0],{"inference/tether":["script/fold"]}]
 
 Using Interface directly ((and without the interpreter condition requiring the unstable --experimental-vm-modules option)#span{color:var(--note);}) as we did to access the sum#i function will have it only use this external context, but every import#b command creates a new internal one that may require our intervention too. Getting to specify Interface as a loader module for Node will have it intercept these contexts with specific procedures: 

loader#script/highlight(` node16 --loader=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./haverbeke_2012_acorn.js;
 node20.2 --import 'data:text/javascript;import("module").then(({register})=>register("./Blik_2023_interface.js",{parentURL:pathToFileURL("./")}));' ./haverbeke_2012_acorn.js;
 node20.7 --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./haverbeke_2012_acorn.js;
 node21.1 --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js --experimental-detect-module ./haverbeke_2012_acorn.js;
`){display:block;width:90%;margin:0px 5%;border-radius:.5em;border:1px solid #43a047;background:black;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;color:#616161;font-size:.8em}
(Module detection introduced and unflagged in 21.1 and 22.7 fails to detect modules with eg. export statements far in the file, so a classic package.json entry may still be needed to ensure module syntax:)#span{display:block;padding:0 5%;color:var(--note);}
(cat package.json 2>/dev/null || echo {} | jq '.type="module"' > package.json;)#span{display:block;white-space:nowrap;overflow:scroll;padding:0 5%;color:var(--note);}

 With the "import" condition, Interface "offloads" this module loading to a secondary thread where it registers#b itself explicitly from the primary one. To this end, a swarm#b record and the subscribe#b and message#b commands are borrowed from a generic module definition of worker#b{}s that allow them to send and receive command delegation#b to and from each other after initialization#b. Once intercepted, the probe#b and prepend#b commands will help imports locate#b modules with unspecified attribution ((author)#b and year#b), while the idempotent peer#b record provide access#b to remote protocols. With the interpreter condition, the Module#b command composes all 3 loading stages explicitly: locate@#___of_Location, access@#___of_Access and interpret@#___of_Interpretation. Without it, Node's import#b procedure limits control to the first two by the names "resolve" and "load", but command#b extends them with custom#b context specification and inference#b. The record of modules#b will store respective modules#script/highlight(`{resolution,resource,module,peer,tests}`){display:inline;word-break:break-all;white-space:normal;font-family:inherit} references by relative specifier for speed and transparency. To further extend itself, location is illustrated in isolation for sources#b, specifying an importmap#b to remote addresses. 

